  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Advanced Github Copilot (GHCP) Lab",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "./README-TS.md",
      "description": "Start Here!",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Start here!"
    },
    {
      "file": "./models.ts",
      "description": "Get to know your data!\r\n\r\nHere we have alarms and user events data.\r\nYou will need to help process this information\r\n\r\nScroll down this page and view the user events.\r\nTake note of the structure - location, names, severity and everything",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Know your data"
    },
    {
      "file": "Completions.ts",
      "description": "Using GHCP completions is optimal for Short, Specific and Simple logic (The 3 `S` principle), remember that!\r\n\r\n\r\nFor best completions results - \r\nName a variable according to the logic you expect and then refactor the name after.\r\n\r\nPro tips:\r\n1. GHCP also suggests names (!) if you press Refactor (F2) on the function/variable name.\r\n2. Watch this for more context on [The 3 `S` Principal](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hh1nOX14TyY&t=38s&ab_channel=VisualStudioCode)",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Completions introduction"
    },
    {
      "file": "./Completions.ts",
      "description": "#1 Let's warm up and let Copilot do it for us, please create a variable to store the total alarm events.\r\n\r\nSimply place your caret after the equal sign and hit space.\r\nLet's see what GC completes.",
      "line": 2,
      "title": "Completions #1"
    },
    {
      "file": "./Completions.ts",
      "description": "#2 Your first attempt at solo assignment, save the number of user events into a variable.\r\n\r\nHowever, look! Github Copilot knows exactly what to do, and offers you to create exactly that variable on your behalf!",
      "line": 5,
      "title": "Completions #2"
    },
    {
      "file": "./Completions.ts",
      "description": "#3 So for your actual solo assignment - create a variable to store the total number of events, user and alarms.",
      "line": 8,
      "title": "Completions #3"
    },
    {
      "file": "./Completions.ts",
      "description": "#4 Create a method to count the total alarm events which happened by an input of location",
      "line": 11,
      "title": "Completions #4"
    },
    {
      "file": "./Completions.ts",
      "description": "#5 Find the list of users names which were at the same location where the alarm events occurred.\r\nDo not input any parameters and let Copilot iterate over the data and find the matching pairs.\r\n\r\nShould return an array of user *names*, example: [\"Brad\", \"Lee\", \"Cooper\"]",
      "line": 14,
      "title": "Completions #5"
    },
    {
      "file": "./Completions.ts",
      "description": "#6 Create a dictionary with keys of alarm severities and a count of how many alarm events of respective severity type events occurred.\r\n\r\nExample output:\r\n{ \r\n    Low: 2,\r\n    Medium: 3,\r\n    High: 3\r\n}",
      "line": 17,
      "title": "Completions #6"
    },
    {
      "description": "# 🆕 From Prompt Engineering to Context Engineering\r\n\r\n## The New Standard: AGENTS.md\r\n\r\nVS Code now supports **two formats** for repository-wide custom instructions:\r\n\r\n1. **AGENTS.md** (NEW - recommended format)\r\n   - Place at the root of your workspace\r\n   - Now **generally available** and enabled by default\r\n   - Supports **nested AGENTS.md files** in subfolders (experimental via `chat.useNestedAgentsMdFiles` setting)\r\n   - Provide context-specific instructions for different parts of your codebase (e.g., frontend vs backend)\r\n\r\n2. **.github/copilot-instructions.md** (legacy format)\r\n   - Still supported for backward compatibility\r\n   - Consider migrating to AGENTS.md for better support\r\n\r\n## Context Engineering in Practice\r\n\r\n### Utilize VS Code workspaces\r\n\r\n1. Setup your Copilot instructions for test creation, code generation, code-reviews and even commit messages.\r\n2. Commit the VS Code workspace in your repo and instruct your team to use it for development.\r\n3. Instructions come in the form of free text in markdown files. The more context you enrich Github Copilot with, the better results you'll get.\r\n\r\n### Nested AGENTS.md Example\r\n```\r\nproject-root/\r\n├── AGENTS.md              # General project guidelines\r\n├── frontend/\r\n│   └── AGENTS.md          # Frontend-specific instructions\r\n└── backend/\r\n    └── AGENTS.md          # Backend-specific instructions\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip:** The more contextual and specific your instructions, the better Copilot performs. This is why we call it **Context Engineering** over Prompt Engineering!\r\n\r\nSee [docs](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/configure-custom-instructions/add-repository-instructions#creating-repository-wide-custom-instructions)",
      "pattern": "github.copilot.chat.commitMessageGeneration.instructions",
      "title": "🆕 Context Engineering with AGENTS.md"
    },
    {
      "file": "InlineChat.ts",
      "description": "Using GHCP Inline Chat fits scenarios where you need more than one thing at a time, a very specific logic that's too long for a variable name, or you want to provide examples (yes, that's a recommendation!).\r\n\r\nIn VS Code the default shortcut is ctrl + I to open inline chat.",
      "line": 72,
      "title": "Inline chat introduction"
    },
    {
      "file": "./InlineChat.ts",
      "description": "Create a method which returns a list of the users' name property if the alarm and user events overlap in time (exactly).\r\nDuring the comparison, if there's a match, console log the alarm name and user name",
      "line": 74,
      "title": "Inline chat 1"
    },
    {
      "file": "./InlineChat.ts",
      "description": "Did you copy-paste this? If the answer is yes, how will you practice phrasing your requirements?\r\n\r\nIf the thought of typing a lot is what deters you I have just the solution - talk to computer! \r\n\r\nVoice to text options: \r\n1. \"VS Code Speech\" extension (Also recommended in workspace and should be installed) you can simply talk to VS Code with speech to text. This works very well and can be done both in editor, in inline chat as well as in the chat window, give it a go!\r\n1. Windows Key + H - built in live transcribing tool.\r\n1. Handy (https://handy.computer) all local on your computer.",
      "line": 75,
      "title": "Inline chat — speech tip"
    },
    {
      "file": "./InlineChat.ts",
      "description": "Create method which creates a dictionary of alarm events by alarm name, unless the name contains intrusion (Casing insensitive). In the value store the event locations as a list.\r\n\r\nExample: \r\n{\r\n    'Gas Leak': ['Kitchen', 'Bathroom'],\r\n    ...\r\n}",
      "line": 76,
      "title": "Inline chat 2"
    },
    {
      "file": "./InlineChat.ts",
      "description": "Now let's talk about comments vs inline chat?\r\n\r\nWhy not comments? They are also left in the repo explaining what the code below does!\r\n\r\n  1. Code gets refactored, comments usually do not. These comments will demand a cleanup in the future and get disregarded and become irrelevant.\r\n  2. More importantly, comments with GHCP aren't iterable, meaning it's a one-shot completions attempt. If you get it wrong, you have to edit your comment and try again. When using the inline chat you can then reply with what needs to be fixed. Just think of how rare it is when you code things that work for the first time, the better you are explaining what you want to GHCP, the better GHCP will help you, sometimes it takes a few rounds of explanations, a.k.a. \"inline chat ;)\"",
      "line": 77,
      "title": "Inline chat — comments"
    },
    {
      "file": "Chat.ts",
      "description": "Using GHCP Chat is ideal for brainstorming and ideation, questions and reassurance of your code. \r\nWith GCHP chat (CTRL + ALT + I), it's much easier to provide more context using ALIASES, we'll talk about aliases next.\r\nThe more precise your context is, the more capable Copilot would be.\r\n\r\n\r\nSome examples one can use. Select your text, or multiple texts then use #selection.\r\n1. \"Check if I missed any edge cases in #selection\"\r\n1. \"Can you provide a numerical example for #selection\"\r\n1. \"How do I run the project?\" More often than not, the instructions may be a little obscure, Copilot knows how to simplify and provide simple commands.\r\n1. Use it for git operations! \"What command to edit the last commit message?\"\r\n\r\nRemember - context is everything, if GHCP chat starts providing bad information, it may be confused with older contexts or has context rot.\r\n\r\nDon't ask about multiple projects in the same chat, create a new chat for each subject and maintain coherent context.\r\n\r\nYou can always navigate to previous sessions.\r\n",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Chat introduction"
    },
    {
      "file": "Chat.ts",
      "description": "Open the panel (Again, Ctrl+Alt+I) and let's get to know our aliases \"`@`\", tools and context accessors \"`#`\" and operations \"`/`\".\r\n\r\nStart typing each of these and see what operations show up in the pop up completion, take the time and iterate with Up and Down arrows. Read a little to know about the possibilities these provide, they are very useful.",
      "line": 2,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 5,
          "character": 347
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "./Chat.ts",
      "description": "Open up Copilot (Ctrl + Shift + I) and make it prompt it, to get a list the `#AlarmEvent` keys.\r\n\r\nCopilot should chat list out the keys in the chat. \r\n\r\nIf you can't find the definition... Consider skipping this one and coming back to it after we talk about #codebase.\r\n",
      "line": 3,
      "title": "Chat 1"
    },
    {
      "file": "./Chat.ts",
      "description": "Using Copilot, ask it where you can find the `UserEvent` interface?\r\n\r\nExpected result, get a reference to `models.ts`",
      "line": 5,
      "title": "Chat 2"
    },
    {
      "file": "./Chat.ts",
      "description": "Somewhere in the code, I've listed several SECRET keys. Why don't you try and ask Copilot to list them for you? \r\nYou may use #codebase to list them, give it a try.\r\n\r\nThere are variations and typos to the secrets. Find all isn't an option - but Copilot still finds these!",
      "line": 7,
      "title": "Chat 3"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Real-World Application: AI-Assisted Merge Conflict Resolution",
      "description": "# 🆕 Resolve Merge Conflicts with AI\r\n\r\n**New in VS Code 1.105**: When you open a file with git merge conflict markers, VS Code now offers AI-powered conflict resolution!\r\n\r\n## How It Works\r\n\r\n1. **Automatic Detection**: When merge conflicts are detected, a new action appears in the lower right-hand corner of the editor\r\n2. **AI Analysis**: Click the action to open the Chat view\r\n3. **Context-Aware Resolution**: The AI analyzes:\r\n   - The merge base (common ancestor)\r\n   - Changes from your current branch\r\n   - Changes from the incoming branch\r\n4. **Review & Customize**: You can review the proposed resolution and provide additional context if needed\r\n\r\n## Customization with AGENTS.md\r\n\r\nYou can customize the merge conflict resolution behavior using an `AGENTS.md` file in your repository. This allows you to:\r\n- Define team-specific merge strategies\r\n- Add project conventions for conflict resolution\r\n- Specify preferred resolution patterns\r\n\r\n## When to Use This\r\n\r\n✅ **Great for:**\r\n- Complex merge conflicts with multiple changes\r\n- Understanding what both branches were trying to accomplish\r\n- Getting suggestions when you're unsure which changes to keep\r\n\r\n⚠️ **Remember:**\r\n- Always review AI suggestions carefully\r\n- Test the resolved code before committing\r\n- You have the final say - the AI provides suggestions, not decisions\r\n\r\n**Note**: This feature explains and demonstrates what's available. During the workshop, we'll mention this exists and how to access it when you encounter merge conflicts in real projects."
    },
    {
      "file": "models.ts",
      "description": "Introducing Agent mode — iterative agent-driven workflows that can orchestrate multi-file changes with iterations.\r\n\r\nLet's ask Copilot to create the spec for our functionality:\r\n\r\n> _I want the main function to use the different functions that exists in the codebase to allow filtering specific alarms based on inputs._\r\n> \r\n> _Create a console app that generates alarms every three seconds in the background._\r\n>\r\n> _Without interfering with alarm generation, the user will be able to get the current status by choosing a number from the menu which will execute a specific function._\r\n\r\nPaste the prompt, and take the response.",
      "line": 8,
      "title": "Github Copilot Agent 🤯"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Plan Before You Code: The Plan Agent",
      "description": "# 🆕 The Plan Agent - Your AI Planning Assistant\r\n\r\n**New in VS Code Insiders**: The built-in **Plan Agent** helps you prepare for complex coding tasks before writing a single line of code!\r\n\r\n## Why Plan First?\r\n\r\nJumping straight into code can lead to:\r\n- ❌ Missing important requirements\r\n- ❌ Overlooking edge cases\r\n- ❌ Inefficient architecture decisions\r\n- ❌ Rework and refactoring\r\n\r\nThe Plan Agent helps you **analyze tasks, break them down into steps, and generate implementation plans** before development.\r\n\r\n## How to Use the Plan Agent\r\n\r\n1. **Open Chat View**: Press `Ctrl+Alt+I`\r\n2. **Select Plan Agent**: Click the agents dropdown and select **Plan** or simply use `/Plan`\r\n3. **Describe Your Task**: Explain what you want to build\r\n4. **Review the Plan**: The agent will:\r\n   - Analyze requirements\r\n   - Break down into logical steps\r\n   - Identify potential challenges and ask clarifying questions\r\n   - Suggest implementation approach\r\n5. **Next Steps**: \r\n   - Start implementation immediately\r\n   - Save the plan for later\r\n   - Refine the plan with follow-up questions\r\n\r\n## Example Workflow\r\n\r\n```\r\nYou: /plan Create a user authentication system with JWT tokens\r\n\r\nPlan Agent:\r\n1. Analysis of requirements\r\n2. Database schema design\r\n3. JWT token generation logic\r\n4. Authentication middleware\r\n5. Error handling strategy\r\n6. Security considerations\r\n...\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Integration with Handoffs\r\n\r\nThe Plan Agent uses the new **handoff feature** - after finalizing your plan, you'll see buttons to transition to the next mode with the plan context preserved!\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Complex features benefit most from planning. Use this for:\r\n- New feature development\r\n- Architecture refactoring\r\n- Integration projects\r\n- Performance optimization tasks"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 🚀 SUPER IMPORTANT: Delegate Research with Subagents",
      "description": "# 🆕 Isolated Subagents - The Game Changer\r\n\r\n**New in VS Code Insiders**: **Subagents** let you delegate autonomous tasks to specialized AI agents within your chat sessions - and this is HUGE for managing context!\r\n\r\n## The Context Problem\r\n\r\nEver noticed how your chat sessions get \"bloated\" with information?\r\n- Research about different approaches\r\n- API documentation lookups\r\n- Code analysis from multiple files\r\n- All this fills up your main context window\r\n\r\n## How Subagents Solve This\r\n\r\nSubagents operate with **their own separate context window** and work **autonomously without user interaction**. \r\n\r\nThey:\r\n\r\n✅ **Complete their task independently**\r\n\r\n✅ **Return results to your main chat**\r\n\r\n✅ **Keep your main context clean**\r\n\r\n✅ **Don't pollute your conversation history**\r\n\r\n\r\n## Using Subagents\r\n\r\n### In Chat Prompts\r\n```\r\nRun #runSubagent tool for context gathering and research about auth mechanisms for this app.\r\n```\r\n\r\n### In Custom Chat Modes\r\nYou can reference `#runSubagent` in your custom mode definitions to automatically delegate research tasks.\r\n\r\n### Ideal Use Cases\r\n- 🔍 **Research**: \"Investigate all authentication patterns used in #codebase\"\r\n- 📊 **Analysis**: \"Analyze performance bottlenecks across all services\"\r\n- 📚 **Context Gathering**: \"Find all error handling approaches in the project\"\r\n- 🔎 **Code Discovery**: \"Locate all database migration files and summarize changes\"\r\n\r\n## Why This Is SUPER IMPORTANT\r\n\r\n**Before Subagents:**\r\n```\r\nYour Chat:\r\n[1000 tokens of research]\r\n[500 tokens of API docs]\r\n[800 tokens of code analysis]\r\n[Your actual question]\r\n❌ Context limit reached!\r\n```\r\n\r\n**With Subagents:**\r\n```\r\nYour Chat:\r\n[Your actual question]\r\nSubagent Result: [Clean, summarized findings]\r\n✅ Context optimized!\r\n```\r\n\r\n## The Power of Delegation\r\n\r\nThink of subagents as **specialist consultants** - they do the deep dive research and come back with concise answers, leaving your main conversation focused on the actual problem you're solving.\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Combine subagents with the Plan Agent for maximum efficiency - let subagents gather context while the Plan Agent structures your implementation approach!\r\n\r\n**Availability**: VS Code Insiders only (as of version 1.105)\r\n\r\nLearn more: [Subagents Documentation](https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/chat/chat-sessions#_subagents)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Managing All Your Agent Work: The Agent Sessions View",
      "description": "# Agent Sessions View - Now Inside the Chat Panel\r\n\r\nThe **Agent Sessions View** has moved — it now lives **directly inside the Chat panel**, so you can see your active session and all your agent runs side by side without switching panes.\r\n\r\n## How to Open It\r\n\r\n- **Option 1**: Open the Chat panel (`Ctrl+Alt+I`) and click the **Sessions** view tab inside it.\r\n- **Option 2**: Use the Command Palette (`Ctrl+Shift+P`) and search for **\"Agent Sessions\"**.\r\n\r\n## All Four Agent Types in One Place\r\n\r\nThe Sessions View gives you visibility into all the different ways agents can run:\r\n\r\n| Agent Type | Description |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| 🖥️ **VS Code (Local)** | Your current local chat and inline agent sessions |\r\n| 💻 **GitHub Copilot CLI** | Terminal-based agent sessions |\r\n| ☁️ **Cloud (Background)** | GitHub Copilot Coding Agent running remotely on your behalf |\r\n| 🔄 **Background Agent** | Long-running tasks delegated to run in the background |\r\n\r\nAll four are visible and manageable from within the same Sessions View — no need to track them across separate tools.\r\n\r\n## What You Can Do\r\n\r\n- **Track progress** of all active and recent agent runs\r\n- **Resume sessions** — pick up where any agent left off\r\n- **Review results** — see what each agent did without losing your current conversation context\r\n\r\n## Summary: The Complete Agent Ecosystem\r\n\r\nYou've now learned about:\r\n1. **@plan Agent** - Strategic planning\r\n2. **Subagents** - Focused research and analysis\r\n3. **Agent Sessions View** - Managing it all from within Chat\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Because the Sessions View is side by side with your active chat, you can monitor a background cloud agent while continuing to work locally — no context switching required!"
    },
    {
      "title": "Create a Custom Chat Mode: PRD builder",
      "description": "Let's set up our own custom GitHub Copilot Chat Mode to accelerate creation of Product Requirements Documents (PRDs).\n\n**Goal**: Create a mode named **PRD builder** that loads the persona instructions so you can quickly draft structured PRDs from short prompts.\n\n### Steps\n1. Open the Copilot Chat panel (Ctrl + Alt + I).\n1. Click the mode dropdown (where you pick *Ask* / *Agent*). You probably noticed there are already two custom modes, let's create our own by selecting **Configure modes** (see screenshot below).\n\n    ![Configure Modes](./assets/ConfigureModes.png)\n\n1. In the configuration UI, click **Create a new custom mode**.\n\n    ![Create Mode](./assets/Create a new custom mode.png)\n\n1. Let's use the User level scope to share this across projects instead of being project specific\n\n    ![User Data Folder](./assets/UserDataFolder.png)    \n        \n1. Name it exactly: `PRD builder`.\n1. Open this file `PRD-Builder-Persona.md` ([link](./personas/PRD-Builder-Persona.md)) and copy its entire contents into the new mode's definition/instructions text area.\n1. (Optional) Pick an emoji/icon to make it stand out (🧱, 📄, 🚀).\n1. Save the mode.\n\nCongratulations, you've created a custom mode.\n\n\n### What This Gives You\n- Consistent PRD structure (intro, goals, user stories, requirements, non-goals, metrics)\n- Automatic reminder to ask clarifying questions first\n- Faster onboarding for teammates who can reuse the same mode\n\n### Pro Tips\n- You can store multiple personas (e.g., *Test Plan Builder*, *API Spec Writer*) the same way.\n- Version your persona files in Git so the team can improve them collaboratively.\n- If you update the persona text here, re-open the mode config and re-paste to sync.\n\nWhen you're done, try giving it a *very short* feature idea and see how it first asks you clarifying questions (that's by design!).\n\nGive it a spin—your future specs will thank you. ✨"
    },
    {
      "title": "Chat Mode Handoffs: Orchestrating Multi-Step Workflows",
      "description": "**Handoffs** are a powerful feature that enable you to create guided, sequential workflows that transition between different chat modes with suggested next steps.\n\n### What Are Handoffs?\nHandoffs appear as interactive buttons after a chat response completes, allowing users to seamlessly move to the next mode with:\n- Relevant context preserved from the previous step\n- A pre-filled prompt tailored to the next workflow stage\n- Optional auto-submission for fully automated flows\n\n### The Power of Orchestration\nWe've built a complete workflow for feature development:\n\n1. **PRD Builder Mode** - Start with a brief feature idea\n   - AI asks clarifying questions\n   - Creates a detailed Product Requirements Document\n   - ↓ *Handoff button: \"Generate Tasks\"*\n\n2. **PRD to Tasks Mode** - Transform the PRD into actionable tasks\n   - Analyzes functional requirements\n   - Generates high-level parent tasks\n   - Breaks down into detailed sub-tasks\n   - ↓ *Handoff button: \"Process Tasks\"*\n\n3. **Process Tasks Mode** - Execute the implementation\n   - Helps implement each task step-by-step\n   - Provides code generation and guidance\n   - Validates implementation against PRD\n\n### Why This Matters\n- **No Context Loss** - Information flows seamlessly between modes\n- **Guided Experience** - Users follow a proven workflow without jumping between tools\n- **Iterative Refinement** - Each step builds on the previous one\n- **Reduced Cognitive Load** - One-click transitions vs manual copy-paste\n\n### Try It Out\nYou can see this orchestration in action by:\n1. Opening a custom chat mode file (e.g., `PRD-2🎬.chatmode.md`)\n2. Looking at the `handoffs` section in the YAML frontmatter\n3. Notice how each mode defines its next step\n\n**Pro Tip**: You can chain multiple handoffs together to create even longer workflows—imagine: Planning → Design → Implementation → Testing → Documentation!"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Plan the Alert System with /plan",
      "description": "# 🆕 The /plan Agent: Your Planning Copilot\r\n\r\n**New in v1.109** – The `/plan` agent brings structured planning right into your workflow!\r\n\r\n## Let's Build an Alert System\r\n\r\nOpen the Chat panel (`Ctrl+Alt+I`) and type:\r\n\r\n```\r\n/plan I want to build a full alert management system with 3 components: an alert generator that creates randomized alerts, a classifier that categorizes them by severity and location, and a console UI for viewing and filtering alerts.\r\n```\r\n\r\n## The 4-Phase Workflow\r\n\r\nWatch as the agent guides you through:\r\n\r\n1. **🔍 Discovery** – Analyzes your requirements and existing codebase\r\n2. **🤝 Alignment** – Asks clarifying questions (e.g., \"Should alerts persist? What filtering options?\")\r\n3. **📐 Design** – Proposes architecture, file structure, and implementation approach\r\n4. **✨ Refinement** – Iterates based on your feedback until you approve\r\n\r\n## Why This Matters\r\n\r\n✅ **No more jumping straight into code** – Think before you build\r\n✅ **Catch issues early** – The agent identifies edge cases and design risks\r\n✅ **Team alignment** – Share the plan for review before implementation\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: The `/plan` agent won't commit to a design until you've answered its clarifying questions. This collaborative approach prevents costly rework!\r\n\r\n🎯 Give it a try now – see how planning transforms your development workflow."
    },
    {
      "file": "../.github/skills/generate-alerts/SKILL.md",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "🆕 Agent Skills: Create /generate-alerts",
      "description": "# 🆕 Agent Skills: Reusable AI Commands\r\n\r\n**Now GA in v1.109** – Agent Skills let you create custom slash commands backed by natural language instructions!\r\n\r\n## Explore the Skill File\r\n\r\nYou're looking at a **SKILL.md** file. Notice the structure:\r\n\r\n1. **YAML Frontmatter** – Metadata (name, description, version)\r\n2. **Markdown Body** – Natural language instructions for Copilot\r\n\r\n## How Skills Work\r\n\r\n✨ **On-Demand Loading** – Copilot only reads the full instructions when your request matches the skill name. This keeps context windows clean!\r\n\r\n🎯 **Universal Format** – The same skill works across:\r\n- VS Code Chat\r\n- GitHub Copilot CLI\r\n- GitHub Coding Agents\r\n\r\n## Try It Now\r\n\r\nOpen Chat (`Ctrl+Alt+I`) and type:\r\n\r\n```\r\n/generate-alerts 10\r\n```\r\n\r\nWatch as the skill generates 10 realistic alert events using the instructions defined in this file!\r\n\r\n## The agentskills.io Standard\r\n\r\nSkills follow the open **Agent Skills** standard (see [agentskills.io](https://agentskills.io)). This means:\r\n- Portable across AI tools\r\n- Version-controlled in Git\r\n- Shareable across teams\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Build a library of skills for common tasks like `/run-tests`, `/deploy-staging`, `/generate-docs`. Your team's collective knowledge becomes reusable AI commands! 🚀"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Bootstrap Instructions with /init",
      "description": "# 🆕 The /init Command: Auto-Generate AI Instructions\r\n\r\n**New in v1.109** – Let Copilot analyze your project and write comprehensive workspace instructions for you!\r\n\r\n## What Is /init?\r\n\r\nThe `/init` command is like having an expert technical writer analyze your codebase and document how AI should interact with it.\r\n\r\n## Try It Now\r\n\r\n1. Open Chat (`Ctrl+Alt+I`)\r\n2. Type: `/init`\r\n3. Watch the magic happen! ✨\r\n\r\n## What It Does\r\n\r\nCopilot will:\r\n\r\n🔍 **Analyze your project structure** – Detects frameworks, languages, and patterns\r\n📚 **Discover existing AI conventions** – Finds AGENTS.md, custom modes, skills\r\n📝 **Generate instructions** – Creates or updates `copilot-instructions.md` with:\r\n- Project overview and architecture\r\n- Coding conventions and style guides\r\n- Build, test, and deployment commands\r\n- Framework-specific best practices\r\n\r\n## Perfect For\r\n\r\n✅ **New projects** – Prime Copilot with project context from day one\r\n✅ **Onboarding** – Help new team members (and AI!) understand your codebase\r\n✅ **Refactoring** – Update instructions when your architecture evolves\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Run `/init` after major architectural changes to keep Copilot's understanding fresh. Commit the generated `copilot-instructions.md` to Git so the whole team benefits! 🎯"
    },
    {
      "file": "../.github/agents/alert-expert.agent.md",
      "line": 67,
      "title": "🆕 Build Specialized Agents",
      "description": "# 🆕 Custom Agent Control: Fine-Tune Agent Behavior\r\n\r\n**Enhanced in v1.109** – Custom agents now support advanced control options in their YAML frontmatter!\r\n\r\n## Explore This Agent\r\n\r\nYou're looking at the **alert-expert** agent. Check out the frontmatter settings:\r\n\r\n```yaml\r\nuser-invocable: true\r\ndisable-model-invocation: false\r\n```\r\n\r\n### What These Mean\r\n\r\n**`user-invocable: true`**\r\n- ✅ Agent appears in the agents dropdown menu\r\n- ✅ Users can manually select it from Chat\r\n\r\n**`disable-model-invocation: false`**\r\n- ✅ Copilot can auto-select this agent when relevant\r\n- ✅ Smart routing based on conversation context\r\n\r\n## Compare: Manual-Only Agents\r\n\r\nNow open `../.github/agents/ui-builder.agent.md` and notice:\r\n\r\n```yaml\r\ndisable-model-invocation: true\r\n```\r\n\r\n💡 This means **manual selection only** – Copilot won't auto-invoke it. Great for specialized tools that need explicit human approval!\r\n\r\n## New in v1.109: Additional Controls\r\n\r\n**`agents:`** – Limit which subagents this agent can invoke\r\n```yaml\r\nagents: ['research-agent', 'code-generator']\r\n```\r\n\r\n**`model:`** – Specify fallback model preferences\r\n```yaml\r\nmodel: ['claude-sonnet-4', 'gpt-4']\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Try It Live\r\n\r\n1. Open Chat (`Ctrl+Alt+I`)\r\n2. Click the **agents dropdown** (top of chat panel)\r\n3. Select **alert-expert**\r\n4. Ask: \"What are the best practices for severity classification in alert systems?\"\r\n\r\nNotice how the agent responds with specialized knowledge based on its instructions! 🎯\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Use `user-invocable: false` for \"internal\" agents that should only be called by other agents, never directly by users."
    },
    {
      "file": "../.github/agents/orchestrator.agent.md",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "🆕 Wire an Agent Orchestra",
      "description": "# 🆕 Agent Orchestration: The Conductor Pattern\r\n\r\n**New in v1.109** – Create conductor agents that delegate to specialized subagents!\r\n\r\n## The Orchestrator Agent\r\n\r\nYou're looking at an **orchestrator** – a meta-agent that manages other agents. Check the frontmatter:\r\n\r\n```yaml\r\nagents: ['alert-expert', 'ui-builder']\r\n```\r\n\r\n### What This Does\r\n\r\n🎼 **Restricts delegation** – This agent can ONLY invoke `alert-expert` and `ui-builder`, not other agents\r\n\r\n🎯 **Enforces architecture** – Prevents uncontrolled agent sprawl and keeps workflows predictable\r\n\r\n## The Conductor Pattern\r\n\r\nInstead of having one mega-agent that does everything:\r\n\r\n❌ **Anti-pattern**: Single \"do everything\" agent\r\n```\r\n[User] → [Mega Agent: planning, coding, testing, UI, backend, DevOps...]\r\n```\r\n\r\n✅ **Best Practice**: Conductor + Specialists\r\n```\r\n[User] → [Orchestrator]\r\n          ├→ [Alert Expert]: Domain logic\r\n          ├→ [UI Builder]: Frontend\r\n          └→ [Test Agent]: Validation\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Try It Now\r\n\r\n1. Open Chat (`Ctrl+Alt+I`)\r\n2. Select **orchestrator** from agents dropdown\r\n3. Ask: \"Build the alert generator and a console UI to display the alerts.\"\r\n\r\n### What Happens\r\n\r\n🎭 **The orchestrator analyzes** your request\r\n🤝 **Delegates to alert-expert** for the generator logic\r\n🎨 **Delegates to ui-builder** for the console UI\r\n📦 **Returns coordinated results** from both subagents\r\n\r\n## Why This Architecture Wins\r\n\r\n✅ **Clear separation of concerns** – Each agent has one job\r\n✅ **Reusable specialists** – Other orchestrators can use the same subagents\r\n✅ **Easier to debug** – Problems isolated to specific agents\r\n✅ **Team scaling** – Different people maintain different agents\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Think of orchestrators as project managers and specialists as senior engineers. The orchestrator doesn't code – it coordinates! 🚀"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Parallel Subagents in Action",
      "description": "# 🆕 Parallel Subagents: Work Smarter, Not Slower\r\n\r\n**Game-changer in v1.109** – Subagents can now run **in parallel**, dramatically speeding up complex workflows!\r\n\r\n## The Old Way (Sequential)\r\n\r\nBefore v1.109:\r\n```\r\n[Task 1: Build generator] ⏳ (2 min)\r\n → [Task 2: Build classifier] ⏳ (2 min)\r\n   → [Task 3: Build UI] ⏳ (2 min)\r\n\r\nTotal: 6 minutes\r\n```\r\n\r\n## The New Way (Parallel)\r\n\r\nWith v1.109:\r\n```\r\n[Task 1: Build generator]  ⏳\r\n[Task 2: Build classifier] ⏳  ← All running simultaneously!\r\n[Task 3: Build UI]         ⏳\r\n\r\nTotal: 2 minutes\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Try It Now\r\n\r\n1. Open Chat (`Ctrl+Alt+I`)\r\n2. Select the **orchestrator** agent\r\n3. Ask:\r\n\r\n```\r\nBuild three things simultaneously:\r\n1. Alert generator that creates 10 random alerts per second\r\n2. Severity classifier that categorizes alerts by risk level\r\n3. Console dashboard that displays alerts with filtering\r\n```\r\n\r\n### What You'll See\r\n\r\n✨ **Multiple active spinners** in the chat\r\n📊 **Expandable subagent cards** – Click to see each agent's:\r\n- Current progress\r\n- Tools being used\r\n- Initial prompt\r\n- Real-time status\r\n\r\n🎯 **Clean main context** – Each subagent runs in its own isolated context window\r\n\r\n## The Magic: Context Isolation\r\n\r\nEach subagent has:\r\n- ✅ Its own dedicated context window\r\n- ✅ Independent tool access\r\n- ✅ Separate conversation history\r\n- ✅ No pollution of your main chat\r\n\r\n## When to Use Parallel Subagents\r\n\r\n✅ **Independent tasks** – Generator, classifier, and UI don't depend on each other\r\n✅ **Research phases** – Search docs, analyze code, review tests simultaneously\r\n✅ **Multi-file operations** – Update frontend, backend, tests in parallel\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Watch the status bar at the bottom of VS Code – it shows how many subagents are currently active! 🚀"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Test Harness with Agent Hooks",
      "description": "# 🆕 Agent Hooks: Automate Your Test Harness\r\n\r\n**New in v1.109** – Hooks let you run commands automatically at key points in the agent lifecycle!\r\n\r\n## The Test-First Pattern\r\n\r\nImagine this workflow:\r\n\r\n1. ✍️ Write tests first (TDD)\r\n2. 🤖 Let agent code autonomously\r\n3. ✅ Tests run automatically when agent finishes\r\n4. 🔁 Agent sees failures and self-corrects\r\n\r\nThis is **exactly** what hooks enable!\r\n\r\n## Setup Your Hook\r\n\r\n### Step 1: Open Hooks Config\r\n\r\nIn Chat (`Ctrl+Alt+I`), type:\r\n```\r\n/hooks\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Step 2: Create a Stop Hook\r\n\r\nAdd a new hook that runs when the agent session ends:\r\n\r\n- **Event**: `Stop`\r\n- **Command**: `npx tsc --noEmit` (TypeScript type check)\r\n\r\n### Step 3: Let It Run\r\n\r\nNow when the agent finishes:\r\n- ✅ Your test command runs automatically\r\n- ✅ Agent sees the output\r\n- ✅ If tests fail, agent can iterate and fix\r\n- ✅ No manual intervention needed!\r\n\r\n## Available Hook Events (8 Total)\r\n\r\n| Event | When It Fires |\r\n|-------|---------------|\r\n| `SessionStart` | Agent session begins |\r\n| `Stop` | Agent session ends |\r\n| `PreToolUse` | Before agent calls any tool |\r\n| `PostToolUse` | After agent calls any tool |\r\n| `SubagentStart` | Subagent begins work |\r\n| `SubagentStop` | Subagent completes |\r\n\r\n## Cross-Tool Compatibility\r\n\r\nHooks work in:\r\n- ✅ VS Code (Copilot Chat)\r\n- ✅ Claude Code (Anthropic's editor)\r\n- ✅ GitHub Copilot CLI\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Use `PostToolUse` hooks to automatically format code after edits (`npx prettier --write`), ensuring consistency without manual intervention! 🎯"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Steer the Agent Mid-Task",
      "description": "# 🆕 Message Steering: Control Agents in Real-Time\r\n\r\n**New in v1.109** – Redirect, queue, or interrupt agents while they work!\r\n\r\n## The Problem\r\n\r\nEver had this happen?\r\n\r\n❌ Agent starts implementing feature A\r\n❌ Halfway through, you realize you need to adjust\r\n❌ No way to course-correct without canceling\r\n\r\n**Message Steering fixes this!**\r\n\r\n## How It Works\r\n\r\nWhile an agent response is **in progress**, the Send button becomes a **dropdown** with 3 options:\r\n\r\n### 1️⃣ Steer with Message\r\n✨ **Redirect the agent mid-task** without canceling\r\n\r\nTry it: While the agent builds the alert system, steer with:\r\n```\r\n\"Actually, add color coding by severity:\r\nCritical=red, Error=orange, Warning=yellow, Info=blue\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 2️⃣ Add to Queue\r\n📋 **Queue a message** to send after the current task completes\r\n\r\n### 3️⃣ Stop and Send\r\n⛔ **Cancel current task** and start fresh\r\n\r\n## Advanced: Queue Management\r\n\r\n🔀 **Drag to reorder** queued messages\r\n🗑️ **Remove items** you no longer need\r\n\r\n| Scenario | Action |\r\n|----------|--------|\r\n| Agent on right track, needs adjustment | **Steer** |\r\n| Current task good, add follow-up | **Queue** |\r\n| Agent went completely wrong | **Stop** |\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Use steering for incremental refinement and queuing for sequential tasks. This keeps agents productive without constant supervision! 🚀"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Remember Design Decisions with Copilot Memory",
      "description": "# 🆕 Copilot Memory: Persistent Context Across Sessions\r\n\r\n**New in v1.109** – Copilot can now remember your design decisions **across chat sessions**!\r\n\r\n## Enable Copilot Memory\r\n\r\nOpen VS Code Settings (`Ctrl+,`) and enable:\r\n```\r\ngithub.copilot.chat.copilotMemory.enabled: true\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Teach Copilot Your Conventions\r\n\r\nIn Chat (`Ctrl+Alt+I`), tell Copilot:\r\n\r\n```\r\nRemember that in this project:\r\n- Critical alerts should always be displayed in red\r\n- All alerts use UTC timestamps in ISO8601 format\r\n- Severity distribution: 40% Info, 30% Warning, 20% Error, 10% Critical\r\n- Alert IDs use UUID v4 format\r\n```\r\n\r\n✨ Copilot confirms it will remember these conventions.\r\n\r\n## Test It\r\n\r\n1. **Start a NEW chat session** (`Ctrl+Shift+P` → \"New Chat Session\")\r\n2. Ask: \"Generate 5 sample alert events for testing\"\r\n3. 🎯 **Watch Copilot apply your design decisions** without being reminded!\r\n\r\n## What Gets Remembered\r\n\r\n✅ Design decisions – Color schemes, formatting rules\r\n✅ Architecture choices – \"We use Redux for state management\"\r\n✅ Code style – \"Always use arrow functions\"\r\n✅ Business logic – \"Premium users get 3x alert quota\"\r\n\r\n## Manage Your Memories\r\n\r\nVisit [github.com/settings/copilot](https://github.com/settings/copilot) to view, edit, or delete memories.\r\n\r\n❌ **Never store secrets** – No API keys or passwords!\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: At the start of a new project, have a \"memory bootstrap\" chat where you teach Copilot all your conventions in one session. Every subsequent chat becomes more productive! 🎯"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Visualize with Mermaid Diagrams",
      "description": "# 🆕 Mermaid Diagrams: Interactive Architecture Visualization\r\n\r\n**New in v1.109** – Copilot generates **interactive Mermaid diagrams** directly in chat!\r\n\r\n## Try It Now\r\n\r\nIn Chat (`Ctrl+Alt+I`), ask:\r\n\r\n```\r\nCreate a Mermaid flowchart showing the complete alert system pipeline:\r\nAlert Generator → Severity Classifier → Status Manager → Console UI\r\nInclude decision nodes for severity routing.\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Navigation Controls\r\n\r\n- 🔍 **Alt + Scroll** to zoom in/out\r\n- 🖱️ **Alt + Drag** to pan around\r\n- 🖼️ **Button** to open in full editor\r\n\r\n## More Ideas to Try\r\n\r\n**Sequence diagram**:\r\n```\r\nCreate a Mermaid sequence diagram showing how an alert flows\r\nfrom creation to display: User → Generator → Classifier → UI\r\n```\r\n\r\n**State diagram**:\r\n```\r\nCreate a state diagram for an alert's lifecycle:\r\nNew → Active → Acknowledged → Resolved → Archived\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Real-World Use Cases\r\n\r\n✅ Architecture discussions before coding\r\n✅ PR descriptions with visual diffs\r\n✅ README documentation\r\n✅ Onboarding new team members\r\n\r\nThe generated Mermaid code is **plain text** — paste it into GitHub READMEs, Notion, Confluence, or any Markdown tool that supports Mermaid.\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Start broad (\"flowchart of the system\"), then refine (\"add error handling paths\"). Copilot iterates on diagrams just like code! 🚀\r\n\r\n**Learn more**: [mermaid.js.org](https://mermaid.js.org)"
    },
    {
      "file": "EasterEggs.md",
      "description": "From this list of emails below, I only want the non-domain part formatted with new line separators",
      "line": 14
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Plugins & Marketplace — Share Your Copilot Stack",
      "description": "So far you've customized Copilot with **custom agents**, **skills**, and **prompts** — but only for yourself or this project.\r\n\r\n💡 **What if your whole team could install the same agents and skills with a single command?**\r\n\r\nThat's exactly what the **Copilot CLI Plugin Marketplace** enables:\r\n\r\n- 📦 **Plugin** = a bundled package of agents + skills + MCP configs\r\n- 🏪 **Marketplace** = a Git repo that lists installable plugins\r\n- 🔗 **Distribution** = anyone with repo access installs with one command\r\n\r\nThis is how teams share Copilot customizations **internally** — no manual file copying, no documentation to follow, just one install command.\r\n\r\nLet's walk through how this repo is set up as a marketplace 👇"
    },
    {
      "file": "../.github/plugin/marketplace.json",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "The Marketplace Manifest",
      "description": "The heart of any plugin marketplace is a single file: **`.github/plugin/marketplace.json`**\r\n\r\nCopilot CLI looks for this file when you register a repo as a marketplace. It lists every plugin available to install, along with metadata for browsing.\r\n\r\nThis repo's marketplace (`ghcp-lab-marketplace`) lists 3 plugins:\r\n- `alert-toolkit` — alert analysis agent + triage skill\r\n- `code-reviewer` — code review agent + checklist skill\r\n- `learning-companion` — tutor agent + practice exercise skill\r\n\r\nEach `source` field is the path to the plugin directory, relative to the repo root.\r\n\r\n�� **Team tip**: Host this file in your org's internal tooling repo so everyone can browse and install the same curated agent set."
    },
    {
      "file": "../plugins/alert-toolkit/plugin.json",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Inside a Plugin",
      "description": "Each plugin is a directory with a **`plugin.json`** manifest at its root.\r\n\r\nThis tells Copilot CLI:\r\n- The plugin's name and version\r\n- Where to find its **agents** (`.agent.md` files)\r\n- Where to find its **skills** (`SKILL.md` files)\r\n\r\nThe structure is identical to your project's `.github/agents/` and `.github/skills/` — the only difference is it's **packaged and installable**.\r\n\r\n🎯 Anything you can put in `.github/agents/` or `.github/skills/`, you can put in a plugin and share across your team."
    },
    {
      "file": "../plugins/alert-toolkit/agents/alert-analyst.agent.md",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Bundling Agents into a Plugin",
      "description": "Agent files inside a plugin are **identical** to the agent files you've seen throughout this lab — same `.agent.md` format, same YAML frontmatter.\r\n\r\nOnce the plugin is installed, this agent is available in every Copilot CLI session automatically:\r\n```sh\r\ncopilot plugin install alert-toolkit@ghcp-lab-marketplace\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen just use it:\r\n```\r\nAnalyze these alerts for anomaly patterns\r\n```\r\n\r\n⚡ **Precedence**: Project-level agents (`.github/agents/`) always win over plugin agents with the same name — so teams can always override a shared plugin locally."
    },
    {
      "file": "../plugins/alert-toolkit/skills/triage-alerts/SKILL.md",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Bundling Skills into a Plugin",
      "description": "Skills inside a plugin use the same `SKILL.md` format and are invoked the same way — with `/skill-name` in a session.\r\n\r\nAfter installing `alert-toolkit`, the skill is immediately available:\r\n```\r\n/triage-alerts from recent incidents\r\n```\r\n\r\n📦 **What a plugin can bundle:**\r\n- **Agents** — domain experts, workflow automation\r\n- **Skills** — slash commands for repeatable tasks\r\n- **MCP server configs** — connect to APIs, databases, tools\r\n- **Hooks** — auto-run actions on events\r\n\r\nSee `plugins/` at the repo root for all 3 example plugins in this lab."
    },
    {
      "title": "⚠️ Registering a Marketplace",
      "description": "⚠️ **Important**: Marketplace registration runs in a **regular terminal — NOT inside a Copilot CLI session**.\r\n\r\nOpen PowerShell / bash outside the CLI, then:\r\n\r\n**1 — Register the marketplace:**\r\n```sh\r\n# From a GitHub repo\r\ncopilot plugin marketplace add OWNER/ghcp-lab\r\n\r\n# Or from a local clone (use an absolute path)\r\ncopilot plugin marketplace add C:\\path\\to\\ghcp-lab\r\n```\r\n\r\n**2 — Browse available plugins:**\r\n```sh\r\ncopilot plugin marketplace browse ghcp-lab-marketplace\r\n```\r\n\r\n**3 — Install a plugin:**\r\n```sh\r\ncopilot plugin install alert-toolkit@ghcp-lab-marketplace\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou only register once. After that, every plugin in that marketplace is always available to install."
    },
    {
      "title": "Using Installed Plugins",
      "description": "Once installed, plugin agents and skills appear alongside your project's own customizations — automatically.\r\n\r\nVerify installation (outside CLI):\r\n```sh\r\ncopilot plugin list\r\n```\r\n\r\nInside a Copilot CLI session, confirm they loaded:\r\n```\r\n/agent\r\n/skills list\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Then use them naturally in any session:**\r\n```\r\nAnalyze these alerts for patterns\r\n/triage-alerts from the last 24 hours\r\n```\r\n\r\n🏢 **Ship this to your team in 3 steps:**\r\n1. Create a shared repo with your agents, skills, and `marketplace.json`\r\n2. Share one command: `copilot plugin marketplace add YOUR-ORG/copilot-tools`\r\n3. Everyone installs the plugins they need — same tools, consistent behavior\r\n\r\n📖 [Copilot CLI Plugin Reference](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/cli-plugin-reference)"
    },
    {
      "file": "EasterEggs.md",
      "description": "Congratulations!!!\r\n\r\n\r\nYou have completed the lab and a few easter eggs, I hope that you have learned a thing or two and had some fun while you're at it.\r\n\r\nRemember - \r\n\r\n\r\nShort.\r\n\r\n\r\nSimple.\r\n\r\n\r\nSpecific.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAnd provide the relevant context (#file/ #selection, etc.)",
      "line": 27
    }
  ],
  "ref": "main"
